TI  - Understanding cyberspace as a security threat: Perceptions of a sample of South Africans
PY  - 2022
T2  - Peace, Safety and Security: African Perspectives
SP  - 331
EP  - 352
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85145643417?origin=resultslist
AD  - University of KwaZulu Natal, Durban, South Africa
AD  - Owerri, Nigeria
AB  - In this era of unequalled resources and technological evolution, cybersecurity has become a growing concern globally. Technological vulnerabilities have led to massive data breaches in recent years and research has highlighted potential uses of artificial intelligence to engineer more powerful cyber-attacks thus revealing new hardware weaknesses. Cyber-attacks pose a threat to critical infrastructure thereby compelling countries to intensify their national security testing for cross-border partnerships. South Africa, however is falling behind in terms of readiness and capacity building. Currently, the country has the world's third-highest number of cybercrime victims, according to the South African Banking Risk Information Centre (SABRIC) losing over R2.2bn annually to cyber-attacks. In October 2019, two seemingly separate groups of hackers threatened to close down the finance sector and local government at a time when South Africans should be paying municipal bills and gain access to their bank accounts. With growing legislation focussing on cybersecurity, this study argues that there is a need for an institute to deliver academic services to the society and conduct research-led activities to ensure South Africa is resilient to the growing threat. Consequently, using the survey design, this investigation discovered that the level of awareness of cybercrimes among the selected population in South Africa is very low, thereby necessitating the establishment of centres of learning, where citizens can be educated on these malevolent activities. With the drive towards the digital economy and future smart cities, there is the need for an underpinning of cybersecurity to ensure the safety and security of the national infrastructure, society, and the economy from malicious online activities. © Peter Lang GmbH.
